    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["U1", "U2", "U6", "U10"],
      "pins": ["U1.19", "U1.20", "U1.18", "U1.21", "U1.22", "U2.5", "U6.5", "U10.5", "U2.6", "U6.6", "U10.6"],
      "nets": ["NET0010 (SCK)", "NET0011 (SO)", "NET0019 (CS)", "NET0009 (CS)", "NET0017 (CS)"],
      "observed": "On mcu.kicad_sch the shared SCK net (NET0010, tied to pin 5/SCK of all three MAX31855 chips U6/U2/U10) is wired to U1 pin 19 = GPIO4, while U1 pin 20 = GPIO5 carries a chip-select net (NET0009, pin 6 of U2). The other two chip-selects land on GPIO3 (NET0019, pin 18) and GPIO6 (NET0017, pin 21); GPIO7 (pin 22) is left unconnected. SO (NET0011) is on GPIO10 (pin 25).",
      "expected": "The specification states the SPI bus is wired to the ESP32 as 'U1 GPIO5/SCK, GPIO10/SO' with chip-selects 'driven by ESP32 GPIO4/GPIO6/GPIO7 respectively' (confirmed by firmware main.rs: SPI2 on GPIO5/GPIO10, CS GPIO4/6/7). The schematic's SPI nets are all shifted down by one GPIO (SCK on GPIO4 instead of GPIO5, CS on GPIO3/5/6 instead of GPIO4/6/7, GPIO7 unused).",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "SCK (pin 5) and SO (pin 7) ... tied to all three ICs and to the ESP32 (U1 GPIO5/SCK, GPIO10/SO). Each chip has its own independent chip-select ... driven by ESP32 GPIO4/GPIO6/GPIO7 respectively (thermocouples.kicad_sch / controller.net, §4)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "mcu.kicad_sch: U1 pin 19 'GPIO4/ADC1_CH4' -> NET0010; pin 20 'GPIO5/ADC2_CH0' -> NET0009; pin 18 'GPIO3' -> NET0019; pin 21 'GPIO6' -> NET0017; pin 22 'GPIO7' unlabeled. thermocouples.kicad_sch: U6/U2/U10 pin 5 'SCK' -> NET0010, pin 6 'CS' -> NET0019/NET0009/NET0017"}
      ],
      "impact": "The firmware drives SCK on GPIO5 and CS0 on GPIO4, but the board presents SCK on GPIO4 and a CS on GPIO5; the clock and a chip-select are transposed and one channel's CS (expected GPIO7) is never asserted. SPI2 will not communicate with the MAX31855 devices, so all three thermocouple readings fail / the oven cannot run.",
      "confidence": 0.7
    },
    {
      "category": "open_connection",
      "components": ["D6"],
      "pins": ["D6.2"],
      "nets": ["NET0004"],
      "observed": "The WS2812B status LED D6 has its DIN (pin 2) connected only to global label NET0004, which appears nowhere else on any sheet; no ESP32 GPIO pin carries NET0004, so the data line is floating.",
      "expected": "The specification describes 'a single onboard WS2812B status LED (D1)' driven by the MCU to expose status (firmware led.rs). Its data input must connect to an ESP32 GPIO.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "Exposes status over Wi-Fi via a small webserver ... and a single onboard WS2812B status LED (D1). (§1 TL;DR)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "mcu.kicad_sch: D6 WS2812B pin 2 'DIN' -> NET0004 (single-use global label, no matching MCU pin)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The status LED cannot be driven; the board loses its visual run-state indication.",
      "confidence": 0.5
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The thermocouple SPI bus is wired to the wrong ESP32 GPIOs (off by one: SCK on GPIO4 instead of GPIO5, CS on GPIO3/5/6 instead of GPIO4/6/7, GPIO7 unused), contradicting the firmware pin map the spec confirms, and the WS2812B data line (NET0004) is left disconnected from the MCU; both would make the board behave differently from the intended design."
}

